-#         cells, then repeats.
-#       - Collision geometry is unchanged; only the color laid on each painted step changes.
+#       - in the top-left region, trails use AABB
+#       - in the opposite region, trails use ABAB
+#       - when a beam crosses the partition, the new region restarts its own
+#         cycle from phase 0 at the first painted cell after the crossing
 #   behavior_trace:
 #     example:
-#       - Original: a red source emitted cells R R R R ... regardless of turns.
-#       - Variant: the same source emits R R G G R R G G ... if its frame is green.
-#       - If a 90-degree collision blanks the meeting cell, that blank cell does not
-#         consume a phase slot; both beams continue from the next painted cell with the
-#         same cycle position they would have had without that missing pixel.
-#       - If a 45-degree collision paints one resolving cell for each participant, each
-#         beam advances its cycle by exactly one painted cell before continuing in zigzag.
+#       - suppose A=red block color and B=green frame color
+#       - before the partition: red, red, green, green, ...
+#       - after the partition: red, green, red, green, ...
+#       - if a 90-degree collision blanks a cell, that missing cell does not
+#         advance the cycle; if a 45-degree collision paints resolving cells,
+#         those cells do advance the cycle exactly once
 #   complexity_calibration:
-#     - The cycle length is fixed at 4 and reused everywhere, so the task stays learnable.
-#     - Using the already-visible frame color avoids introducing a hidden palette lookup.
-#     - The generator filters out samples with no frame-colored trail pixels, preventing
-#       degeneration back into the original continuous single-color behavior.
+#     - only two region behaviors are used, keeping the task learnable
+#     - both region cycles reuse colors already visible in each source object:
+#       A is the inner block and B is the shared frame
+#     - the generator requires at least one cross-region beam, so the fused
+#       behavior is visible and not reducible to the plain D3 variant
 #   implicit_assumptions_review:
-#     - Original `paint_cell()` assumed one fixed color per beam; now color depends on
-#       the beam-local painted-cell index.
-#     - `truncate_beam()` was harmless before because color was constant; now it must
-#       also rewind the future color phase. This is handled by deriving phase from the
-#       current path length instead of storing a separate mutable color pointer.
-#     - Collision handlers still only change movement patterns. They do not overwrite
-#       the color cycle, so each beam keeps its own AABB coloring after turning.
+#     - original `_find_blocks_2x2()` is no longer valid because large region
+#       rectangles contain many accidental monochrome 2x2 patches
+#     - original `is_free()` assumed exactly one traversable background color;
+#       now any region-color cell is traversable
+#     - original `unpaint_cell()` could restore one global background color;
+#       now it must restore each cell's original region color
+#     - D3-style path truncation now also has to restore the beam's local
+#       region-cycle state, because the future color phase depends on the last
+#       surviving painted cell and on which region it belongs to
 #   placement_legality:
-#     - Frames still obey the original non-overlap and clearance constraints.
-#     - The generator still validates inputs by running the solver, so any placement
-#       that would violate collision or blocking assumptions is rejected.
+#     - frames are still non-overlapping 4x4 objects with original gap rules
+#     - every frame is placed wholly inside one partition side
+#     - the generator checks ray clearance against full frames before opening
+#       gaps, then validates the result with the solver
 #   visual_observability:
-#     - Because frame-colored pixels appear outside the frames in the output, the new
-#       behavior is immediately distinguishable from the original task.
-#     - Multiple beams can share one frame color, but each beam also repeats its own
-#       source color, making the pairing A=block, B=frame readable from the source object.
+#     - the partition is a large visible structure in the input, so the switch
